---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Best Surge Protectors for Treadmills (2026): What Manuals Allow";
const description =
  "Three treadmill surge protectors checked against maker and retailer listings: a single-outlet unit, a 15 A and a 20 A plug-in, and why a power strip is wrong.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "panamax-md2",
    name: "Panamax MD2",
    category: "Two-outlet direct plug-in surge protector, 15 A",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Panamax+MD2+2+Outlet+Direct+Plug-In+Surge+Protector&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$45–$75",
    priceBounds: {"low":45,"high":75},
    specs: "2 outlets · 15 A · 1,350 joules · UL 1449 3rd ed., 400 V VPR line to neutral · direct plug-in, no cord · 2 status lights",
    pros: [
      "Plugs directly into the wall with no cord, so there is no strip or extension in the chain",
      "A 400-volt line-to-neutral protection rating, at the ceiling many treadmill manuals set",
      "At 1,350 joules, well above the 450-joule minimum some manuals specify",
      "Cuts power entirely in a catastrophic surge, and its lights show protection and wiring faults",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Only if your manual permits a surge protector, and a two-outlet unit does not meet a manual that asks for a single-outlet one",
      "Not for treadmills with a 20-amp plug",
      "Stock is patchy: one retailer showed it out of stock and another as no longer available",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The pick for a treadmill with a standard plug whose manual allows a surge protector. Use one socket, for the treadmill only.",
    description: "Panamax still lists the MD2 on its own site, and it is our default for a treadmill with a standard 15-amp plug. It plugs straight into the wall, so there is no cord to warm up, and Panamax and an independent dealer agree on its key figures: 15 amps, 1,350 joules, and a UL 1449 third-edition voltage protection rating of 400 volts line to neutral, with 500 volts line to ground. Some listings also show a separate 330-volt UL 1449 rating alongside these; the third-edition figures above are the ones Panamax publishes. Two lights show power and protection and warn of a wiring fault, and Panamax says its circuitry disconnects power in a catastrophic surge rather than passing it through. Panamax offers a $100,000 limited connected-equipment policy, but says it does not apply to internet purchases from dealers it has not authorised. The limits: it has two sockets, so a manual that calls for a single-outlet unit points you to the Tripp Lite instead, and availability varied between retailers on the day we checked.",
  },
  {
    id: "tripp-lite-spikecube",
    name: "Tripp Lite SPIKECUBE",
    category: "Single-outlet direct plug-in surge protector, 15 A",
    badge: "Best Single-Outlet",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Tripp+Lite+SPIKECUBE+1+Outlet+Surge+Protector&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$12–$18",
    priceBounds: {"low":12,"high":18},
    specs: "1 outlet · 120 V, 15 A, 1,800 W · UL 1449 let-through 400 / 500 / 500 V · green protected and red grounded lights · direct plug-in",
    pros: [
      "A true single-outlet unit, matching the wording of manuals that ask for one",
      "Plugs straight into the wall, with nothing else able to share it",
      "Lights show both that protection is active and whether the outlet is grounded",
      "Lifetime limited warranty and $5,000 of connected-equipment insurance in the US, Canada and Puerto Rico",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Only if your manual permits a surge protector; listings do not say which protection mode the 400 V figure belongs to",
      "Has its own 15-amp circuit breaker, which could trip under a heavy treadmill load before the wall breaker does",
      "Joule rating is listed inconsistently, so we left it off this line; check the exact listing",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For a 15-amp treadmill whose manual specifies a single-outlet surge suppressor. If its breaker ever trips, take it out and plug the treadmill straight in.",
    description: "Tripp Lite, now part of Eaton, sells the SPIKECUBE as a one-outlet personal surge protector, and it is the single-outlet device we could verify most fully. Tripp Lite's listing and independent retailers agree that it plugs directly into the wall, has one socket, is rated 120 volts, 15 amps and 1,800 watts, and carries UL 1449 let-through figures of 400, 500 and 500 volts, with a green light for protection and a red one for grounding. The listings do not label which figure applies to which protection mode, so if your manual sets a 400-volt limit, it is worth a call to Tripp Lite to confirm. Tripp Lite and most retailers give it 600 joules, but one distributor lists a lower figure, so we have left joules out of the specification line; every figure we saw was above the 450-joule minimum some manuals set. Two honest caveats. It contains its own 15-amp breaker, which could trip on a big motor load. And it is a small, inexpensive device with patchy stock — one retailer listed it as out of stock on the day.",
  },
  {
    id: "panamax-m2a20",
    name: "Panamax M2A20",
    category: "Two-outlet direct plug-in surge protector, 20 A",
    badge: "Best for 20-Amp Outlets",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Panamax+M2A20+2+Outlet+Direct+Plug-In+Surge+Protector+20+Amp&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$79–$128",
    priceBounds: {"low":79,"high":128},
    specs: "20 A maximum · 1,350 joules · 3 diagnostic lights (ground, line fault, protection) · direct plug-in, no cord",
    pros: [
      "The only direct plug-in protector we could verify with a 20-amp rating",
      "No cord: it plugs straight into the outlet, as a manual-compliant device should",
      "A ground light, a line-fault light and a protection light, so you can see wiring problems",
      "Panamax says its circuitry cuts power entirely in a catastrophic surge",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Many manuals for 20-amp treadmills say to plug straight into the wall; check yours first",
      "Listings disagree on its outlet layout and its UL 1449 rating, so both are left out here",
      "Expensive for what it is, and the cheapest seller listed it as special order with no returns",
    ],
    bottomLine: "Only for a treadmill on a 20-amp outlet whose manual permits a surge protector. Check its sockets against your plug when it arrives.",
    description: "A treadmill with a 20-amp plug cannot go on a 15-amp protector, and the M2A20 is the one 20-amp direct plug-in device we could verify. Panamax and independent dealers agree on a 20-amp rating, 1,350 joules and three diagnostic lights showing ground, line fault and protection status, and Panamax's instructions say to plug it into a 20-amp wall outlet. Beyond that, the sources do not agree. Panamax's web page and its own datasheet give different UL 1449 suppression ratings, retailers describe the two sockets as two 20-amp outlets, one 15-amp and one 20-amp, or two standard outlets, and the warranty and connected-equipment terms differ between Panamax's own documents. We have left all of those out. The instructions also say not to install it unless there are at least 30 feet of wire between the outlet and the panel. Treat it as a specialist device: confirm your manual allows a protector, check the sockets against your treadmill's plug on arrival, and buy from a seller that accepts returns.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Buying Guides", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/guides/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Treadmill Surge Protectors", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-surge-protectors-for-treadmills/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best surge protector for a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For a treadmill with a standard plug whose manual allows one, a direct plug-in protector rated for 15 amps with a UL 1449 listing and a voltage rating of 400 volts or less, such as the Panamax MD2. If the manual asks for a single-outlet unit, choose one, such as the Tripp Lite SPIKECUBE. If it says to plug straight into the wall, use no plug-in device.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I plug my treadmill into a power strip with surge protection?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. A multi-outlet strip with a cord is built for several small loads, often has a small breaker that trips on a motor's start-up current, and its cord acts as an extension cord, which most manuals forbid. If your manual allows surge protection, use a single direct plug-in protector rated for the treadmill's current, with nothing else plugged into it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do treadmill manufacturers recommend surge protectors?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Some do and some do not. Several manuals require a single-outlet UL 1449 listed suppressor with a stated voltage and joule rating. Others say to plug directly into a grounded wall outlet with nothing in between, often on a dedicated circuit. Your manual decides. If it is silent, a short call to the manufacturer's support line will settle the question.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How many joules does a treadmill surge protector need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Some manuals set a minimum, and 450 joules is a figure that appears in several. Beyond that, joules are the least standardised number on the box. Check the UL 1449 listing, the voltage protection rating and the current rating first, and use joules as a tie-breaker between devices that pass those checks. Follow your manual if it gives a figure.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is a dedicated treadmill surge protector?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It usually means a surge protector used for the treadmill alone: a single-outlet, direct plug-in unit rated for the machine's current, plugged straight into a wall outlet with nothing else connected to it. It is different from a dedicated circuit, which is wiring from the panel that serves only the treadmill and is installed by an electrician.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is whole-house surge protection better for a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is the better route when your manual says to plug the treadmill directly into the wall, because it protects without anything between the machine and the outlet. It handles large surges arriving from outside the house but does less against surges generated inside. It is installed at the electrical panel, which is always a job for a licensed electrician.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-10-15",
      dateModified: "2026-10-15",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.name.split(" ")[0] },
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 3,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-surge-protectors-for-treadmills/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-15",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-15",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best surge protector for treadmill",
      "treadmill surge protector",
      "dedicated treadmill surge protector",
      "single outlet surge protector",
      "whole home surge protection",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Our Top Picks", link: "#quick-picks" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-bestsurgeprotectorsfortreadmills" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-bestsurgeprotectorsfortreadmills)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buying Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Surge Protectors <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">for Treadmills</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          A treadmill surge protector is a single, correctly rated device between one wall outlet and one machine, not the power strip under the desk. Here are the three plug-in units that passed our checks, what each is honestly for, and what to do when your manual says to plug straight into the wall.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#quick-picks" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            See the Picks
          </a>
          <a href="#single-vs-strip" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Protector vs Power Strip
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="48" height="48" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight"><a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author" class="text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a></p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 15, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="1.7k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the products below.</strong> We have not tested these
          surge protectors. Ratings, outlet counts and warranty terms were checked on 15 October
          2026 against each maker's own product page, datasheet or instructions, and against at
          least one retailer or reviewer that does not make the product. A figure appears on a
          card only where those sources agree; where they disagreed — on the Panamax M2A20's
          outlet layout and UL rating, and on the Tripp Lite's joule figure — we left it out and
          say so. Prices are the ranges we saw on that date and change often. This is general
          information, not electrical advice: <strong>your treadmill's manual is the
          authority</strong>, and if it says to plug directly into a grounded wall outlet with
          nothing in between, do that and buy nothing on this page. Any work on outlets, circuits
          or the electrical panel is for a licensed electrician. We earn a commission if you buy
          through the Amazon links on this page; it does not change which products we list or
          what we say about them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>


    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <div class="not-prose bg-orange-50 border border-orange-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-3">Our Top Picks</h2>
        <ul class="list-none m-0 p-0">
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Best Overall:</span>
            <a href="#panamax-md2" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Panamax MD2</a>
            <a href="#panamax-md2" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Best Single-Outlet:</span>
            <a href="#tripp-lite-spikecube" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Tripp Lite SPIKECUBE</a>
            <a href="#tripp-lite-spikecube" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Best for 20-Amp Outlets:</span>
            <a href="#panamax-m2a20" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Panamax M2A20</a>
            <a href="#panamax-m2a20" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#quick-picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Our Picks at a Glance</a>
          <a href="#picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The 3 Best Surge Protectors for Treadmills</a>
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. How We Chose These Surge Protectors</a>
          <a href="#manual-first" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Start With the Manual — It Decides</a>
          <a href="#single-vs-strip" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Single-Outlet Protector vs Multi-Outlet Power Strip</a>
          <a href="#reading-specs" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Reading the Box: UL 1449, Voltage Rating, Joules and Amps</a>
          <a href="#twenty-amp" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Treadmills With a 20-Amp Plug or Circuit</a>
          <a href="#whole-home" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Whole-Home Surge Protection: An Option for Your Electrician</a>
          <a href="#gfci-and-trips" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. GFCI Outlets, Tripping and What a Protector Cannot Fix</a>
          <a href="#setup-and-replace" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Setting It Up, and Knowing When to Replace It</a>
          <a href="#who-should-skip" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Who Should Buy Something Else</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">12. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people searching for a treadmill surge protector already own one — the multi-outlet
          strip the television and the router are plugged into — and the honest first answer is
          that it is the wrong device. A treadmill motor is a large, changing load, and a strip
          with a thin cord, a small breaker and five other things plugged into it is how plugs
          melt and breakers trip. The right device, where your manual allows one at all, is a
          single protector rated for the treadmill's current that plugs straight into the wall.
          This page lists the three we could verify, and explains when the better answer is no
          plug-in device at all.
        </p>

        <div id="quick-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Our Picks at a Glance</h2>
<p>
            Three plug-in protectors, each matched to a kind of treadmill plug and a kind of
            manual, and one option that is not a product you plug in at all. If you are not yet
            sure whether your treadmill should have a surge protector, start with our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-surge-protector/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether a treadmill needs a surge protector</a>;
            this page assumes the manual allows one and is about which to buy.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Pick</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Best for</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Why</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Panamax MD2</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Best overall, standard 15-amp plug</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Direct plug-in, 15 A, 1,350 joules, 400 V line-to-neutral rating</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tripp Lite SPIKECUBE</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Manuals that ask for a single-outlet unit</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">One outlet, 15 A, UL 1449 let-through figures from 400 V, under $20</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Panamax M2A20</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmills on a 20-amp outlet</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Rated for 20 A, direct plug-in, three diagnostic lights</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Whole-home surge protection</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Manuals that say plug straight into the wall</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fitted at the panel by an electrician; nothing between treadmill and outlet</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Whatever you buy, it goes into a grounded wall outlet on its own, and the treadmill is
            the only thing plugged into it. No strip, no extension cord, no adapter, and no fan or
            phone charger sharing the second socket.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The 3 Best Surge Protectors for Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            This list is shorter than our usual roundups, and deliberately so. Only three plug-in
            devices met every requirement we set: currently sold, rated for a treadmill's current, plugging
            straight into the wall without a cord, and described consistently by the maker and by a
            seller or reviewer that does not make them. We would rather list three we can stand
            behind than pad the page with strips that should never carry a treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            Read each card's first con before anything else. Every one of these devices is only the
            right purchase if your manual permits a surge protector, and two of them have a limit
            — an outlet count or a plug type — that rules them out for some machines.
          </p>
        </div>
