        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Let the manual decide, then buy one device for one machine.</strong>
            If your manual allows a surge protector and your treadmill has a standard plug, the
            Panamax MD2 is our pick; if it asks for a single-outlet unit, the Tripp Lite SPIKECUBE;
            on a 20-amp outlet, the Panamax M2A20. Plug it straight into the wall and plug nothing
            else into it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If the manual says to plug directly into the wall, do that, unplug during storms, and ask
            a licensed electrician about whole-home protection — never bypass a GFCI or bridge a gap
            with a cord. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-surge-protector/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill surge protection</a>
            explains the reasoning behind every rule on this page.
          </p>
